  "rules": [
    "Mild positive bias overall: model levels tend to run ~0.2–1.0 EUR above realized prices, especially in the mid-to-late horizon (roughly days 10–25). Apply a small downward offset that increases slightly with horizon.",
    "Over-persistence after upswings: following recent rallies/spikes in history, the model extrapolates elevated levels too long; realized prices mean-revert sooner. Damp the forecast slope after strong recent gains (e.g., shrink deviations from the last price toward a short-term mean).",
    "Underestimates drawdowns/volatility: when the true path sells off (notably around days ~18–26 in examples), the model stays too high and too smooth. Add stronger downside adjustment when momentum turns negative and/or widen expected variance.",
    "Late-horizon directional miss: in some cases the final week shows realized re-acceleration higher while the model remains flat-to-down (e.g., Example 3 tail). Introduce a regime check: if market is in a high-volatility/uptrend regime, reduce mean-reversion strength in the far tail; otherwise increase it."
  ]
}
